<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"> <head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Epoxy Garage &amp; Basement Floors in Central MA | MCB</title><meta name="description" content="Epoxy and polyaspartic floor coatings for garages and basements in Central MA: typical costs, grind prep, moisture testing, cure temps and road salt."><link rel="canonical" href="https://mcitybuilders.com/flooring/epoxy-garage-floors"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="Epoxy Garage &amp; Basement Floors in Central MA | MCB"><meta property="og:description" content="Epoxy and polyaspartic floor coatings for garages and basements in Central MA: typical costs, grind prep, moisture testing, cure temps and road salt."><meta property="og:url" content="https://mcitybuilders.com/flooring/epoxy-garage-floors"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Maverick City Builders"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Manrope:wght@200;300;400;500;600;700&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"GeneralContractor","@id":"https://mcitybuilders.com/#business","name":"Maverick City Builders","url":"https://mcitybuilders.com","telephone":"(508) 656-7436","email":"douglas@mcitybuilders.com","address":{"@type":"PostalAddress","streetAddress":"1171 Main St","addressLocality":"Lancaster","addressRegion":"MA","postalCode":"01523","addressCountry":"US"},"geo":{"@type":"GeoCoordinates","latitude":42.4556,"longitude":-71.6731},"sameAs":["https://www.facebook.com/mcitybuilders","https://www.instagram.com/mcitybuilders"]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Flooring","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/flooring"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Epoxy Garage and Basement Floors","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/flooring/epoxy-garage-floors"}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","serviceType":"Epoxy Garage and Basement Floors","url":"https://mcitybuilders.com/flooring/epoxy-garage-floors","provider":{"@id":"https://mcitybuilders.com/#business"},"areaServed":{"@type":"AdministrativeArea","name":"Central Massachusetts"}}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does an epoxy garage floor cost in Central Massachusetts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Typical installed ranges run about $6 to $12 per square foot for a ground, flake-broadcast system with a clear topcoat. A two-car garage usually lands somewhere around $3,000 to $6,000. Heavy crack repair, spalled concrete or a slab that needs moisture mitigation adds to that, and the written estimate itemises each of those separately."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Should I get epoxy or polyaspartic?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Many good systems use both: an epoxy base coat for adhesion and build, with a polyaspartic topcoat for UV stability and scratch resistance. Straight epoxy can yellow in sunlight and takes longer to cure. Polyaspartic cures fast and tolerates cooler temperatures, but it costs more and leaves less working time. The right mix depends on your slab, the season and how you use the space."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why do DIY epoxy kits peel?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually the prep. Acid etching a garage slab rarely opens the surface enough, and it leaves oil and old sealers behind. Kit coatings are also thin. Hot tires soften thin epoxy and pull it up, which is called hot tire pickup. Diamond grinding and a thicker, commercial-grade system are what stop that."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you coat a basement floor that gets damp?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sometimes. We test the slab for moisture vapor first. A slab with high readings needs a moisture-mitigating primer rated for it, and a basement that actually takes in water needs that fixed before any coating goes down. A coating is not a drainage system."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How soon can I park in the garage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends on the product and the temperature. Polyaspartic topcoats usually allow foot traffic within hours to a day and vehicles in a couple of days. Epoxy systems often need closer to five to seven days before you park. In cool shoulder-season weather everything takes longer, and we'll give you the date in writing."}}]}</script><!-- First-touch attribution. Runs before anything else so a click-id is never
